124	
125	## Introduction
126	
127	This guide provides best practices for threat hunting using the ADEO CTI MCP Server tools. Threat hunting is a proactive cybersecurity approach that focuses on searching for malicious activities and threats that have evaded existing security solutions.
128	
129	## Available Tools for Threat Hunters
130	
131	### Reconnaissance & Intelligence Gathering
132	
133	1. **DNS Lookup** (dns-lookup)
134	   - Use this tool to resolve domain names to IP addresses
135	   - Helpful for identifying infrastructure connected to suspicious domains
136	   - Example: Investigating domains from phishing emails or suspicious communications
137	
138	2. **Reverse DNS** (reverse-dns)
139	   - Maps IP addresses back to hostnames
140	   - Useful for understanding the ownership and purpose of suspicious IPs
141	   - Can reveal patterns in attacker infrastructure
142	
143	3. **Domain Information** (domain-info)
144	   - Provides comprehensive information about domains including subdomains
145	   - Essential for understanding the attack surface of a target
146	   - Can reveal potential entry points for attackers
147	
148	### Vulnerability Assessment
149	
150	1. **Host Information** (host-info)
151	   - Provides detailed information about internet-facing systems
152	   - Reveals open ports, running services, and potential vulnerabilities
153	   - Critical for understanding exposure and attack vectors
154	
155	2. **CVE Lookup** (cve-lookup)
156	   - Search for specific vulnerabilities by CVE ID
157	   - Provides detailed information about known vulnerabilities
158	   - Essential for understanding the severity and exploit potential
159	
160	### Threat Analysis
161	
162	1. **Search** (search-host)
163	   - Search for specific internet-connected devices, services, or vulnerabilities
164	   - Perfect for finding patterns across multiple targets
165	   - Use Shodan query syntax to create precise searches
166	
167	2. **Search Count** (search-host-count)
168	   - Get statistics about search results without the full details
169	   - Great for understanding the scale of a threat or exposure
170	
171	3. **Search Tokens** (search-tokens)
172	   - Understand how search queries are interpreted
173	   - Helps in refining and optimizing complex searches
174	
175	### Monitoring & Alerting
176	
177	1. **Create Alert** (create-alert)
178	   - Set up continuous monitoring for new threats or vulnerabilities
179	   - Get notified when new systems matching your criteria appear
180	   - Essential for ongoing threat hunting operations
181	
182	2. **List/Get/Delete Alerts** (list-alerts, get-alert-info, delete-alert)
183	   - Manage your monitoring operations
184	   - Review and refine your alert criteria
185	
186	### Malware Analysis
187	
188	1. **VirusTotal URL Analysis** (vt-url-analysis)
189	   - Check if URLs are associated with malicious activities
190	   - View detailed reports from multiple security engines
191	   - Perfect for validating suspicious links
192	
193	2. **VirusTotal File Analysis** (vt-file-analysis)
194	   - Analyze suspicious files for malware signatures
195	   - Get comprehensive reports from multiple antivirus engines
196	   - Essential for malware investigation
197	
198	3. **VirusTotal Domain/IP Analysis** (vt-domain-analysis, vt-ip-analysis)
199	   - Investigate domains and IPs for malicious associations
200	   - Understand historical reputation and threat intelligence
201	   - Critical for infrastructure analysis
202	
203	## Workflow Examples
204	
205	### Investigating a Suspicious Domain
206	
207	1. Start with dns-lookup to find associated IP addresses
208	2. Use host-info on those IPs to identify services and vulnerabilities
209	3. Check domain reputation with vt-domain-analysis
210	4. Set up an alert to monitor for changes
211	
212	### Proactive Vulnerability Scanning
213	
214	1. Use search-host with specific vulnerability filters
215	2. Investigate interesting findings with host-info
216	3. Look up specific vulnerabilities with cve-lookup
217	4. Monitor critical assets with create-alert
218	
219	### Malware Campaign Analysis
220	
221	1. Analyze suspicious URLs with vt-url-analysis
222	2. Expand investigation to linked domains using domain-info
223	3. Check IP infrastructure with vt-ip-analysis
224	4. Create alerts for related indicators using create-alert
225	
226	## Best Practices
227	
228	1. **Document Your Process** - Maintain clear records of your hunting activities
229	2. **Focus on Patterns** - Look for connections between seemingly unrelated indicators
230	3. **Validate Findings** - Confirm suspicious activity with multiple tools
231	4. **Continuous Monitoring** - Set up alerts for ongoing surveillance
232	5. **Update Knowledge** - Stay current on new threat tactics and techniques
233	
234	## Conclusion
235	
236	Effective threat hunting combines the powerful tools in the ADEO CTI MCP Server with analytical thinking and security expertise. By following the workflows and best practices in this guide, threat hunters can proactively identify and mitigate potential security risks before they become incidents.
